<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matadi–Kinshasa Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Matadi–Léopoldville railway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matadi">Matadi</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a> Railway, built from 1890 to 1898 as the Matadi–Léopoldville railway, links the seaport of Matadi to Kinshasa, bypassing the rapids of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>. Its construction through difficult country cost the lives of many African and Asian labourers. It replaced head porterage and became the main route for the exports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo basin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matadi-kinshasa-railway.jpg" alt="Matadi–Kinshasa Railway" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MONUSCO Photos · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matadi_train_station_(21236934082).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the 1880s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> relied on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> to reach the interior, but between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matadi">Matadi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a> the river is barred for 300 km by the Livingstone Falls. Goods were carried by human bearers, a slow and often fatal system. On 31 July 1887 the Compagnie du Congo pour le Commerce et l'Industrie was incorporated together with its subsidiary, the Compagnie du Chemin de Fer du Congo, and Albert Thys directed the work; the station of Thysville, now Mbanza-Ngungu, was named after him.</p><p>The hardest stretch led the line out of the river's gorges, through the canyon of the M'pozo River and along the Monts de Cristal. Conditions were miserable and medical care lacking: in 1892 an average of one hundred and fifty workers a month died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, dysentery, beriberi and exhaustion, and of 7,000 recruited by the end of that year 3,500 had died or fled. Thys then brought workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; the Barbadians were forced off their ships at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matadi">Matadi</a> at gunpoint, and seven people were killed. The official toll was 1,932 dead, 1,800 Africans and 132 Europeans, and the real figure was likely higher.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The 366 km line, a portage railway, soon proved profitable, chiefly through the transport of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>. It was laid to a gauge of 765 mm and worked by tank engines and later Garratts. From 1923 to 1931 it was rebuilt to a gauge of 1,067 mm on a new alignment by tens of thousands of convicts and forced workers, of whom seven thousand died, and in 1928 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and Portugal exchanged land between the Congo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> for the new route.</p><p>With the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matadi">Matadi</a> the railway became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a>'s main link to the outside world. After a derailment killed 11 people in 2003 it went unused for more than a decade, reopening in September 2015; traffic stopped again in 2020 and resumed in September 2025, with railcars bought from JR Hokkaido. The hard labour of its building is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heart-of-darkness">Heart of Darkness</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-conrad">Joseph Conrad</a>, who saw it while working in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matadi–Kinshasa Railway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Villagers spent weeks in the forest tapping vines, leaving fields untended, while porterage, food requisitions and the building of the <b>Matadi–Léopoldville railway</b>, completed in 1898 at the cost of many lives, added further burdens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
